1: ---
2: name: completion-report
3: description: Produce the smallest user-facing report that preserves every material fact about a task outcome, by 
classifying the terminal state, comparing it against the contract, filtering activity out, and checking that no 
material fact was lost to compression. Use this skill at a task boundary — when work is complete, partial, blocked, or 
failed — whenever the user asks what the outcome was or for a summary of what was accomplished, and whenever an 
execution run ends and its result must be communicated. Reports consequences and evidence, never the execution 
trajectory.
4: metadata:
5:   author: "Giuliano Lemes <giuice@gmail.com>"
6: ---
7: 
8: # Completion Report
9: 
10: Produce the report that preserves the most material state in the least text.
11: 
12: Two rules generate everything else:
13: 
14: > **No material fact may be lost merely because the answer must be concise.**
15: >
16: > **No execution detail survives merely because it happened.**
17: 
18: ## Boundary
19: 
20: This skill only communicates. It does not plan, replan, execute, verify, or repair. If work can still continue, 
control belongs to `execute-plan` or `plan-from-spec` — not here.
21: 
22: It also does not invent evidence. If something was not verified, the report says so.
23: 
24: ## Inputs
25: 
26: From `spec-interview/<slug>/`:
27: 
28: - **the contract** — `SPEC.md` when it exists; otherwise the `Goal` in `PLAN.md` plus the union of the `Done when` 
conditions in the current plan **and** those the ledger records as satisfied — completed tasks leave the plan on 
replan, so part of the contract survives only in their ledger entries;
29: - **`LEDGER.md`** — the record of what became true, with each entry's `Covers`, evidence, and verification label;
30: - **`PLAN.md`** — for the goal statement and any task that never produced a ledger entry.
31: 
32: Without a ledger, reconstruct the material facts from the run before reporting — but say nothing you cannot ground 
in an observation that actually happened.
33: 
34: Never reconstruct the goal from the execution trace. Read the contract.
35: 
36: ---
37: 
38: ## Step 1 — Classify the terminal state
39: 
40: Do this before writing a word.
41: 
42: | Status | Meaning |
43: |---|---|
44: | `COMPLETED` | Every required acceptance criterion is satisfied, with evidence |
45: | `PARTIAL` | A meaningful subset is done; one or more requirements remain unsatisfied |
46: | `BLOCKED` | Cannot continue without external access, information, permission, or a user decision |
47: | `FAILED` | The required state was not reached and no valid continuation is available |
48: | `NO_OP` | The requested state was already true; no change was needed |
49: 
50: `NO_OP` applies when every ledger entry is `no_op`, or when `PLAN.md` carries `Status: no-op` and no tasks — in 
which case its `Already true because` and `Evidence` lines are your whole input. Report it plainly; do not dress an 
already-satisfied goal up as work.
51: 
52: Never upgrade `PARTIAL`, `BLOCKED`, `FAILED`, or unverified work to `COMPLETED` to produce a cleaner answer.
53: 
54: ## Step 2 — Compare the final state to the contract
55: 
56: Ask what the contract requires — not whether the executor said it finished.
57: 
58: Join ledger entries to criteria through each entry's `Covers`. Build this internally; it is scaffolding, not 
output:
59: 
60: ```
61: AC-001  satisfied     verified    integration test passed
62: AC-002  satisfied     verified    build passed
63: AC-003  satisfied     attested    the user confirmed the six accepted invitations
64: AC-004  unsatisfied   —           test database unavailable
65: AC-005  unverifiable  unverified  requires production access
66: ```
67: 
68: `COMPLETED` requires every criterion in the `satisfied` column. Anything else downgrades the status.
69: 
70: **When several entries cover one criterion** — a task that ended `partial` and the later task that finished its 
remainder — judge the criterion on the combined record, not the last entry alone. A later `verified` entry supersedes 
an earlier `unverified` one for the same criterion. An `Unresolved` item that no later entry closed keeps the 
criterion unsatisfied, however many entries came after it. On a run with no SPEC, join on the `Done when` text the 
ledger copied instead of on `Covers`; an entry whose `Restates:` text matches an earlier entry's `Done when` continues 
that same criterion.
71: 
72: `attested` — a person confirmed it — is satisfied. It is weaker than a machine check, so the report names it as 
confirmed rather than measured, but it does not block completion. Otherwise no work outside an automatable domain 
could ever complete.
73: 
74: The report is the projection of **contract × final state × evidence** — not of the executor's summary. This is 
what stops the implementer from being the sole judge of its own success.
75: 
76: ## Step 3 — Select material facts
77: 
78: > Report consequences, not activity.
79: 
80: A fact is **material** when omitting it could change the user's understanding of: what outcome was achieved, how 
behavior changed, whether the contract was met, whether the result was verified, what risk remains, what is still 
incomplete, what the user must do next, or whether compatibility changed.
81: 
82: ```
83: NON-MATERIAL                      MATERIAL
84: opened five files          →      (nothing — omit)
85: ran a search               →      (nothing — omit)
86: retried a command          →      (nothing, unless it changed the outcome)
87: fixed formatting           →      (nothing — omit)
88: modified IUserRepository   →      the repository interface gained an email lookup,
89:                                   so other implementations must add it
90: ```
91: 
92: The filename can follow the consequence for traceability. It never replaces it.
93: 
94: ## Step 4 — Check coverage before compressing
95: 
96: Every material fact gets exactly one disposition: `REPORT`, `MERGE` (fully carried by another sentence), or `OMIT` 
(implied by a reported fact, or has no user-visible consequence).
97: 
98: These may never be silently omitted when present:
99: 
100: ```
101: - an unsatisfied acceptance criterion
102: - a failed verification
103: - an unverified completion claim
104: - changed external behavior
105: - a changed public contract or interface
106: - changed user data
107: - a destructive or irreversible action
108: - an unresolved blocker
109: - remaining risk
110: - required user action
111: - a material deviation from the contract
112: - an assumption that materially affected the result
113: ```
114: 
115: The ledger carries these as `State delta`, `Unresolved`, `Risk`, `User action`, and `Deviation`. Read all five; 
the outcome is not in `State delta` alone.

